course: everyday
lesson: 366
title: Sam is scared: fear is normal
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every morning Sam's stomach goes tight at the corner where the Station Road boys wait by the bus shelter. In the dinner queue Jake shouts at him, and Sam's heart thumps so hard that he drops his whole tray of beans. The beans go everywhere, Jake laughs, and Sam wishes the floor would swallow him up.
explain: Then Jake says «meet me at the gate after school». All afternoon Sam stares at his book and cannot read a single word. That night he dreams he has to fight Jake in front of the whole school, in his pyjamas. On Monday he pretends to have a tummy ache so he won't have to walk past the rec (the park and playing field) where the big boys play.
explain: At tea Grandad says: «Being frightened means you're a coward, lad.» Dad puts his fork down. «No, it doesn't. I was scared stiff at school for a whole year. Fear is your body's alarm. Everybody has one. Brave people are scared too — they just choose what to do next.»
explain: Sam remembers the swimming trip: he was the only boy who wouldn't do the cannonball off the high board, and he felt awful. But he also remembers the wasp on Lily's sandwich. He froze — and then quietly slid the sandwich away with a ruler. Scared and sensible at the same time. Dad grins: «That's my boy.»
fact: Fear is a normal feeling that every person has, children and grown-ups alike.
fact: When we are scared the body gets ready for danger: the heart beats fast, breathing speeds up and the stomach can feel tight or sick.
fact: Fear can make it hard to think, read or sleep, and can bring bad dreams.
fact: Being frightened does not make someone a coward; courage means doing the right thing while feeling afraid.
fact: Pretending to be ill to avoid something only puts the problem off; it does not solve it.
fact: Telling a trusted grown-up about a fear that keeps coming back is the first step to solving it.
fact: Saying no to a risky dare, like a jump you are not ready for, is a sensible choice, not a weakness.
fact: A threat like «meet me at the gate» should be told to a teacher or parent the same day.
fact: Fear often feels much bigger at night and in dreams than it is in the daytime.
fact: Many adults remember being scared at school; talking about it helps children feel less alone.
exercise: What happens to Sam's body every morning at the bus shelter corner? | His stomach goes tight | He suddenly feels hungry | He gets sleepy and yawns | His legs feel much stronger
exercise: Why did Sam drop his tray of beans? | Jake shouted and his heart thumped with fear | The tray was too heavy to carry | Lily pushed him from behind | He wanted to make everyone laugh
exercise: After Jake said «meet me at the gate», why couldn't Sam read his book? | Fear made it hard for him to think | The book was much too hard for him to read | He had forgotten his glasses at home | The teacher had taken the book away
exercise: Why did Sam pretend to have a tummy ache on Monday? | To avoid walking past the big boys at the rec | Because he wanted to watch TV | Because he really had eaten bad beans | To miss a spelling test he hadn't revised
exercise: What did Sam's nightmare show? | How much the threat was worrying him | That he secretly wanted to fight | That he should wear pyjamas to school | That the fight would really happen
exercise: Grandad says being frightened means you are a coward. Is he right? | No, everyone feels fear; courage is acting well while scared | Yes, brave people never feel fear | Yes, but only for boys | No, because cowards never feel fear
exercise: What did Dad say fear is? | The body's alarm that everybody has | A sign that someone is weak and silly | Something only small children ever feel | A feeling that grown-ups grow out of
exercise: How did Sam feel when Dad said he was scared at school too? | Less alone and less ashamed | Even more frightened than before | Angry that Dad had lied to him | Bored by Dad's old school story
exercise: When the wasp landed on Lily's sandwich, what did Sam do that was wise? | Stayed calm and moved the sandwich with a ruler | Swatted the wasp with his bare hand to squash it fast | Screamed and ran out of the room | Threw the sandwich across the table
exercise: Sam wouldn't do the cannonball off the high board. Was that cowardly? | No, saying no to a jump you're not ready for is sensible | Yes, because a dare is a dare | Yes, because the other boys did it | No, because high boards are always banned
exercise: What would have been the wisest thing to do after Jake said «meet me at the gate»? | Tell a teacher or his mum that same day | Go to the gate and fight him | Hide in the toilets until dark | Bring a bigger friend to hit Jake
exercise: Why doesn't pretending to be ill really help Sam? | The big boys are still there the next day | Because tummy aches are never real | Because Mum will notice | Because the rec will be closed
exercise: Which of these is a kind of courage Sam showed? | Admitting to Dad that he was frightened | Shouting back at Jake in the dinner queue | Doing the cannonball to show off | Walking past the rec without looking
exercise: Who is the best person for Sam to tell about the bus shelter boys? | His mum, dad or a teacher | Nobody, he should keep it secret | Only his pet goldfish at home | The bus shelter boys themselves
exercise: Why did fear feel worst for Sam at night? | Worries feel bigger in the dark and in dreams | Because Jake lives in his house | Because the night time makes the danger more real | Because he hadn't eaten all of his tea that evening
exercise: What did Dad mean by «brave people are scared too»? | Courage is choosing well even while afraid | Brave people pretend they're never scared | Being scared is the same as being brave | Brave people stop feeling fear after age ten
test: «haha Sam dropped his beans cos Jake looked at him, what a baby». Fair? | No, fear makes anyone jumpy, it's not babyish | Yes, only babies get scared | Yes, Sam should've thrown the beans right at Jake's face | No, Sam dropped them on purpose
test: Jake: «gate. 3:15. you're dead». What should Sam do with that? | Tell a teacher or his mum before home time | Turn up at the gate and just get it over with | Keep it secret so nobody calls him a grass | Laugh it off and forget it
test: «just hit him back, Sam, then he'll leave u alone innit». Best answer? | No, I'll tell an adult and stay away from him | OK, I'll hit him first tomorrow | OK, but only if he hits me twice | I'll bring my big brother tomorrow to do it for me
test: Grandad, grumpy: «In my day lads didn't get scared». What's true? | Everybody gets scared, then and now | Children were braver in the old days | Only modern children feel fear | Grown-ups were never scared at school
test: «mum i've got a tummy ache again, can i stay home» — 3rd Monday running. What's probably going on? | Something at school is worrying him and he needs to talk | He has a serious illness only on Mondays | He has eaten beans every Sunday and they make him ill | He just likes Mondays at home
test: «Sam's a chicken, won't even do the cannonball lol». Who's being sensible? | Sam, for not jumping when he wasn't ready | The boys, because everyone must jump | Nobody, because swimming trips are pointless anyway | The lifeguard, for letting them laugh
test: Teacher: «Sam, you've read nothing all afternoon. Anything wrong?» What's best for Sam to say? | «Jake said he'd get me at the gate, I'm scared» | «No, Miss, I'm just thick» | «Nothing, I'm fine, Miss» | «Jake's dead after school, Miss»
test: wasp on ur sandwich, everyone screaming. what's the move | Stay calm and slowly move the food away | Slap it hard with your hand | Scream and wave your arms about until it goes | Blow on it till it flies off
test: «I had a dream I fought Jake in my PJs in front of everyone». What does a dream like that usually mean? | Something is worrying you a lot | It's going to happen for real at school tomorrow | You really want to fight | You need new pyjamas
test: «does ur dad ever get scared?» What's most likely true? | Yes, grown-ups get scared too | No, adults never feel fear about anything | Only if they're cowards | Only in films
test: Mum: «You've gone the long way round all week, love. Why?» Best answer for Sam? | Tell her about the boys at the bus shelter | Say he likes walking further | Say nothing at all and quickly change the subject | Say the bus shelter is haunted
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the emotion of fear in children of primary school age? | It is a normal response experienced by every person | It is an indication of deficient character | It is confined to children who are unwell or otherwise of a nervous disposition | It is absent in those of a courageous disposition
exam: Identify the principal physiological manifestations of fear as observed in the pupil hereinafter referred to as Sam. | An accelerated heartbeat and a tightened stomach | A reduction in heart rate together with marked drowsiness | An increase in appetite and calmness | Improved concentration upon reading matter
exam: Given that a pupil has been summoned by another pupil to «meet at the gate» after school, identify the most appropriate course of action. | To inform a teacher or parent on the same day | To attend the meeting in order to resolve matters | To feign illness on every subsequent school day | To recruit additional pupils to retaliate
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the relationship between fear and courage. | Courage consists in acting rightly notwithstanding fear | Courage is the complete absence of fear | Fear and courage cannot exist together | Courage requires the acceptance of every dare that is offered
